'' A STATIC LIBRARY'S MEMBERS ARE NAMED AFTER THEIR SOURCES. An intermediate object's
'' name is invisible when it is linked and deleted -- but an ARCHIVE keeps it, as the
'' member name. Measured on these two sources:
''
''     fbc    __fb_ct.inf  thing.o  other.o
''     tiko   libmembers.a.1.o      libmembers.a.2.o
''
'' so 'ar t' on ours named the output and an index where fbc names the source. The
'' member names live in the archive's own headers and in nothing tiko prints, which is
'' why this needs EXPECT-FILE-HAS rather than EXPECT-OUT.
''
'' NOT ASSERTING __fb_ct.inf. That is fbc's module-constructor table, a separate
'' artefact this does not produce; naming it here would be asserting something that has
'' never been claimed.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -lib tests/driver/src/thing.bas tests/driver/src/other.bas -o libmembers.a
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-FILE: libmembers.a
'' EXPECT-FILE-HAS: libmembers.a :: thing.o
'' EXPECT-FILE-HAS: libmembers.a :: other.o
'' THE TWO ABOVE ARE THE WHOLE TEST. An EXPECT-NOT for 'libmembers.a.1.o' would read
'' well and assert nothing: EXPECT-NOT matches tiko's STDOUT, where neither the old
'' name nor the new one ever appears. Under the old naming the archive held
'' libmembers.a.1.o and libmembers.a.2.o and no thing.o at all, so the positive
'' assertions are what fail -- verified by building the previous driver and running
'' this against it.
